---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---

<Base
  page="privacy"
  title={`Privacy — ${site.name}`}
  description="What Mise stores, what it asks for, and what it never collects."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-title">Privacy</h1>
  </header>

  <article class="legal-prose">
    <p data-about-line>
      Mise is a macOS menu-bar app. It runs on your Mac. It does not have an account system, and it
      does not include analytics, advertising, or crash reporters.
    </p>

    <h2 data-about-line>What Mise stores</h2>
    <p data-about-line>
      Sets are stored as JSON on this Mac, in Application Support. Each Set is a name plus a list of
      frames: the app, the display, and the position and size as fractions of that display.
    </p>
    <p data-about-line>
      Preferences (gap, shortcuts, whether to show a Dock icon) live in the app’s standard defaults.
      Nothing in that file is a document, a browsing history, or a keystroke log.
    </p>

    <h2 data-about-line>Accessibility</h2>
    <p data-about-line>
      Moving and resizing other apps’ windows goes through macOS Accessibility. Mise asks for that
      permission the first time it needs it, not at launch, and opens the exact System Settings pane
      if it is missing. The permission is used to read window frames and to write them back. It is
      not used to observe typing, to scrape other apps’ contents, or to run in the background as a
      recorder.
    </p>

    <h2 data-about-line>What Mise does not collect</h2>
    <ul data-about-line>
      <li>No telemetry, usage stats, or unique identifiers sent anywhere.</li>
      <li>No cookies, no website account, no mailing list.</li>
      <li>No network calls in the current release. There is no Mise server.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 data-about-line>Mise Pro</h2>
    <p data-about-line>
      Pro is a one-time unlock on your Mac. License state stays on the device. There is still no
      Mise account and no sync server — Sets never leave the Mac they were captured on. If config
      sync ships later, it will be opt-in, and this page will name the path it uses.
    </p>

    <h2 data-about-line>This website</h2>
    <p data-about-line>
      The marketing site does not set cookies. There is no banner because there is nothing to
      consent to on that front. The Mac app still does not phone home.
    </p>
    <p data-about-line>
      Page views and Core Web Vitals go to Vercel Analytics and Speed Insights. Visitors are
      identified by a hash of the request that resets daily, not by a cookie, and the data is stored
      as aggregates. It is not used to reconstruct a session or to follow anyone across sites. See
      Vercel’s notes on
      <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/privacy-policy">Web Analytics</a>
      and
      <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/privacy-policy">Speed Insights</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 data-about-line>Contact</h2>
    <p data-about-line>
      Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
